WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.940
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:06.287
Building the Attribute-Driven Migration.

3
00:00:06.287 --> 00:00:09.950
This is Chapter 2: Graph clients for GCC.

4
00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:15.346
Every piece of code in this workbook that
calls Microsoft Graph should follow one

5
00:00:15.346 --> 00:00:20.505
pattern: know which cloud it's in, send its
token nowhere else, ask for no more than

6
00:00:20.505 --> 00:00:25.663
each call needs, and keep working when
Graph says slow down or a beta API changes.

7
00:00:25.663 --> 00:00:30.135
We'll sort out which cloud is which,
run Microsoft's own snippets to see

8
00:00:30.135 --> 00:00:34.763
what they leave out, walk the client's
rules, then two labs and the dangers.

9
00:00:34.763 --> 00:00:40.280
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

10
00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:46.130
Why does this chapter matter so much?

11
00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:49.876
Because every other chapter's
code talks to Graph or to Azure,

12
00:00:49.876 --> 00:00:53.397
and a client that gets the cloud
wrong, or asks for too much,

13
00:00:53.397 --> 00:00:56.468
carries that mistake into
everything built on it.

14
00:00:56.468 --> 00:01:01.269
So the goal is a client that reaches the
right cloud, asks for the right token,

15
00:01:01.269 --> 00:01:06.380
makes only the calls it declares, and pages
and retries the way Microsoft says to.

16
00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:10.992
Just as important, for each rule in the
code you should be able to say whether

17
00:01:10.992 --> 00:01:15.380
it's Microsoft's contract, Microsoft's
sample, or the program's own design.

18
00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:17.380
Let's start with the clouds.

19
00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:24.805
Get this right first, because
every other rule depends on it.

20
00:01:24.805 --> 00:01:28.660
A GCC Moderate tenant uses
the worldwide endpoints,

21
00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:32.605
the same as a commercial
tenant: Learn says so plainly.

22
00:01:32.605 --> 00:01:38.303
Office 365 GCC is paired with Microsoft
Entra ID in Azure, the commercial cloud,

23
00:01:38.303 --> 00:01:41.634
so tokens come from the
worldwide sign-in host,

24
00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:45.930
and Graph and Resource Manager
are the worldwide hosts too.

25
00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:50.047
The slide maps the two names the
code knows, worldwide and GCC,

26
00:01:50.047 --> 00:01:53.917
including the PowerShell
environment names you'll pass.

27
00:01:53.917 --> 00:01:57.980
They resolve to the same values,
but each is its own name.

28
00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:05.636
The target estate for this
migration is Microsoft 365

29
00:02:05.636 --> 00:02:09.455
GCC with commercial Azure,
so let's make that concrete.

30
00:02:09.455 --> 00:02:12.817
Nothing in its configuration
is a dot-u-s host.

31
00:02:12.817 --> 00:02:17.115
A run that reads both Graph and
Resource Manager needs two tokens,

32
00:02:17.115 --> 00:02:20.617
because one token request
can only name one resource.

33
00:02:20.617 --> 00:02:25.091
And the code still calls the cloud "gcc"
by name, even though it resolves to

34
00:02:25.091 --> 00:02:30.370
the worldwide endpoints, so the run's
evidence says which cloud it was meant for.

35
00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:37.558
This chapter is the reference
for clients that already exist.

36
00:02:37.558 --> 00:02:40.070
Chapters 1, 3 and 4 were written first,

37
00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:44.594
and each has its own Graph client
that follows part of the pattern.

38
00:02:44.594 --> 00:02:48.819
They name the clouds three different
ways: the program's short names,

39
00:02:48.819 --> 00:02:53.345
Learn's PowerShell environment name,
and the names Chapter 5's tool takes.

40
00:02:53.345 --> 00:02:55.407
Each is fine within its own tool.

41
00:02:55.407 --> 00:02:57.169
What isn't fine is a default.

42
00:02:57.169 --> 00:03:01.739
A cloud name a client doesn't recognize
has to stop the run, because quietly

43
00:03:01.739 --> 00:03:06.750
falling back to a default is how a run
ends up talking to a cloud nobody chose.

44
00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.212
Here's what Microsoft gives you.

45
00:03:12.212 --> 00:03:17.193
Learn's page on national clouds for the
Graph SDKs names the three things a client

46
00:03:17.193 --> 00:03:20.018
has to change per cloud:
where it gets its token,

47
00:03:20.018 --> 00:03:23.512
where Graph lives, and any
scope that names the Graph host.

48
00:03:23.512 --> 00:03:29.482
Its example connects to a national cloud,
not the worldwide service GCC uses;

49
00:03:29.482 --> 00:03:32.799
for GCC, all three are
the worldwide values.

50
00:03:32.799 --> 00:03:35.813
Other Learn pages cover
paging and throttling.

51
00:03:35.813 --> 00:03:40.764
The Python examples live in Microsoft's
snippets repository, so the workbook

52
00:03:40.764 --> 00:03:45.401
pins an unmodified copy of the two files
it uses, with a hash a test checks.

53
00:03:45.401 --> 00:03:48.982
A test then runs them against
stand-ins that record exactly

54
00:03:48.982 --> 00:03:52.490
what they'd sign in to,
without signing in to anything.

55
00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:00.097
Running the snippets against the
stand-ins shows what they leave to you,

56
00:04:00.097 --> 00:04:02.777
and the first half is about
cloud and credentials.

57
00:04:02.777 --> 00:04:05.142
The surprise is the Python credential:

58
00:04:05.142 --> 00:04:09.500
on the same page where the other
languages set the authority, the Python

59
00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:14.153
example doesn't, and Python's identity
library falls back to the public cloud.

60
00:04:14.153 --> 00:04:18.665
That happens to be right for GCC,
but it's a default nobody chose.

61
00:04:18.665 --> 00:04:22.987
There's also a cloud written into the
code instead of recorded for the run,

62
00:04:22.987 --> 00:04:26.517
a certificate snippet that
hard-codes the worldwide scope,

63
00:04:26.517 --> 00:04:29.970
and a client secret, which is
just a password in a file.

64
00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.207
The second half is about running safely.

65
00:04:36.207 --> 00:04:39.445
The snippets have no Resource
Manager endpoints at all.

66
00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:41.926
Nothing ties a call to
the permission it needs,

67
00:04:41.926 --> 00:04:45.583
though Learn asks for the least
privileged permission for each one.

68
00:04:45.583 --> 00:04:48.249
There's no paging in the Python snippets,

69
00:04:48.249 --> 00:04:53.582
the SDK won't retry throttled items inside
a batch, and there's nothing about beta.

70
00:04:53.582 --> 00:04:58.355
So the verdict: use Learn's page as the
model for the three things a client sets,

71
00:04:58.355 --> 00:05:01.005
which for GCC are all
the worldwide values,

72
00:05:01.005 --> 00:05:04.035
and for background jobs
use the hardened client,

73
00:05:04.035 --> 00:05:09.320
because a reviewer will ask which calls,
with which permissions, in which cloud.

74
00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:14.370
Now the code itself.

75
00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:17.286
Everything about a cloud
comes from one table:

76
00:05:17.286 --> 00:05:20.663
ask for a cloud by name and
you get its token authority,

77
00:05:20.663 --> 00:05:25.345
its Graph host and its Resource Manager
host, and anything else is an error.

78
00:05:25.345 --> 00:05:28.283
The client won't send its
token to any other host.

79
00:05:28.283 --> 00:05:31.333
Then there's the call list,
one entry per Graph call,

80
00:05:31.333 --> 00:05:36.245
with the least privileged permission from
the API page and the tasks that use it.

81
00:05:36.245 --> 00:05:39.547
Two rules follow: a task
can make only its own calls,

82
00:05:39.547 --> 00:05:43.333
and its permissions are exactly
the union of those calls.

83
00:05:43.333 --> 00:05:46.580
Add one without the other,
and the build fails.

84
00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:52.350
The third rule is the token check,

85
00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:55.268
and it runs before a live
job makes a single call.

86
00:05:55.268 --> 00:05:59.280
An app-only token lists its
application permissions in one claim,

87
00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:02.179
so the Python client reads
it from its own token.

88
00:06:02.179 --> 00:06:04.917
If a permission is missing, the run stops.

89
00:06:04.917 --> 00:06:07.680
If there's one the task
doesn't use, it warns,

90
00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.930
because an unused permission
is still exposure.

91
00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:13.964
PowerShell is trickier,
since the Graph SDK

92
00:06:13.964 --> 00:06:17.167
signs in for you and never
hands over the token.

93
00:06:17.167 --> 00:06:20.860
It turns out the session's context
reports the same permissions,

94
00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:24.560
read from the same claim, so
the module checks that instead.

95
00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:31.810
Paging and throttling are
where clients quietly break.

96
00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:35.448
When Graph returns a next link,
the client follows it to the end,

97
00:06:35.448 --> 00:06:39.727
and it sends the same headers on every
page, because Learn says they aren't

98
00:06:39.727 --> 00:06:43.935
carried forward by default, and an
advanced query changes without them.

99
00:06:43.935 --> 00:06:48.047
When Graph says slow down, the
client waits as long as it's told.

100
00:06:48.047 --> 00:06:52.236
When it isn't told, which really
happens for Conditional Access,

101
00:06:52.236 --> 00:06:53.686
it backs off, with a cap.

102
00:06:53.686 --> 00:06:59.060
It also marks its requests as low priority,
so background reads give way to people.

103
00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:03.360
And inside a batch, it retries
throttled items itself.

104
00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:12.647
Beta APIs can change or disappear, and
Learn says not to use them in production.

105
00:07:12.647 --> 00:07:15.942
But sometimes the only place
a property lives is beta,

106
00:07:15.942 --> 00:07:18.861
like the Autopilot status
this workbook needs.

107
00:07:18.861 --> 00:07:20.702
So beta calls must be declared,

108
00:07:20.702 --> 00:07:25.342
they're read through a wrapper that turns
a missing shape into "not evaluated"

109
00:07:25.342 --> 00:07:29.026
rather than a failed run, and the
stage is always in the output,

110
00:07:29.026 --> 00:07:32.990
so a reader of the evidence
knows which results rest on beta.

111
00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:37.802
Who is this for?

112
00:07:37.802 --> 00:07:41.242
Developers who build any code
in this workbook that calls

113
00:07:41.242 --> 00:07:44.753
Graph or Resource Manager, and
the reviewers who approve it.

114
00:07:44.753 --> 00:07:48.847
You'll want to read Python or PowerShell,
and know what an access token is;

115
00:07:48.847 --> 00:07:51.277
you don't need a tenant
until the second lab.

116
00:07:51.277 --> 00:07:54.062
The reviewer's question
is always the same:

117
00:07:54.062 --> 00:07:57.403
which calls, with which
permissions, in which cloud.

118
00:07:57.403 --> 00:07:58.914
The call list answers it.

119
00:07:58.914 --> 00:08:02.312
And the app registration
itself is part of the answer:

120
00:08:02.312 --> 00:08:06.635
exactly the task's permissions, a
certificate or a managed identity,

121
00:08:06.635 --> 00:08:10.230
never a secret, registered
in the tenant's own cloud.

122
00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:18.705
Lab 1 needs only a laptop, with
Python and PowerShell, and no tenant.

123
00:08:18.705 --> 00:08:22.505
First you run Microsoft's own
snippets against the stand-ins.

124
00:08:22.505 --> 00:08:27.507
Then you print the endpoints for this
estate, and see that nothing for GCC is a

125
00:08:27.507 --> 00:08:32.430
dot-u-s host, and that a cloud name the kit
doesn't know is an error, not a default.

126
00:08:32.430 --> 00:08:35.261
Then you list a task's
permissions from its calls,

127
00:08:35.261 --> 00:08:37.455
and try a call the task never declared.

128
00:08:37.455 --> 00:08:40.200
It's refused before anything is sent.

129
00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:47.062
The rest of the lab runs the
same rules in PowerShell.

130
00:08:47.062 --> 00:08:50.848
An undeclared beta call is
refused, and so is an address on

131
00:08:50.848 --> 00:08:54.713
any host but the cloud's Graph
host, which won't get the token.

132
00:08:54.713 --> 00:08:59.840
Finally you run both suites, eighty-six
Python tests and fifty-two in Pester,

133
00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:03.479
and you've seen every row of
the gap table for yourself,

134
00:09:03.479 --> 00:09:06.060
with no module, tenant or network.

135
00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:14.372
Lab 2 moves to a test tenant, never
production, with fictional data.

136
00:09:14.372 --> 00:09:18.810
It uses the Conditional Access export
because it needs just one permission.

137
00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:21.606
You register the app in
the tenant's own cloud,

138
00:09:21.606 --> 00:09:25.460
with a certificate, grant that
one permission, and run the task.

139
00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:29.554
Then you watch the token check do
its job: leave a permission out and

140
00:09:29.554 --> 00:09:33.722
the run stops before any call, grant
an extra one and you get a warning.

141
00:09:33.722 --> 00:09:38.322
Optionally, run the same export from
PowerShell and compare the two folders.

142
00:09:38.322 --> 00:09:42.770
Then back it all out, because the
output holds the tenant's policies.

143
00:09:46.520 --> 00:09:48.995
Each of these dangers
has a guard in the code.

144
00:09:48.995 --> 00:09:53.818
The two that bite this estate first
are treating GCC as a dot-u-s cloud,

145
00:09:53.818 --> 00:09:57.608
and a cloud name that silently
falls back to a default.

146
00:09:57.608 --> 00:10:01.323
The most dangerous is a
token sent to the wrong host,

147
00:10:01.323 --> 00:10:04.332
so every address, on
every page, is checked.

148
00:10:04.332 --> 00:10:08.050
Others creep in slowly:
permissions the job doesn't need,

149
00:10:08.050 --> 00:10:12.575
a call added without its permission,
headers dropped after page one,

150
00:10:12.575 --> 00:10:17.060
retries that never stop, or a
beta property renamed overnight.

151
00:10:20.810 --> 00:10:22.160
Here's what done looks like.

152
00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:27.080
On the cloud side: an explicit cloud
name that fails on anything unknown,

153
00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:29.832
no dot-u-s hosts in the GCC configuration,

154
00:10:29.832 --> 00:10:33.418
the right authority and
scope for every credential,

155
00:10:33.418 --> 00:10:38.422
one token per resource, and the token
never leaving the cloud's Graph host.

156
00:10:38.422 --> 00:10:43.256
On the run side: every call declared
with its least privileged permission,

157
00:10:43.256 --> 00:10:45.316
an app that holds exactly those,

158
00:10:45.316 --> 00:10:49.911
paging and retries done Microsoft's
way, and beta declared and labeled.

159
00:10:49.911 --> 00:10:54.447
Next, Chapter 3 uses these rules
to write a validation kit check.

160
00:10:54.447 --> 00:10:55.610
Thanks for watching.
